 ( { Black has just played ...Rc2-h2, creating the threat of } 1... Rh3 { with a transposition to a pawn ending where the active king would decide. How should White react? } ) 
1. { -KEY- } Re1 $1 { With the active plan of Rg1-g6, never mind the loss of the b3-pawn. Even if this loses (ten years after the inclusion of this example to the book, Stockfish signals that it indeed does!), in a practical situation that is what we have to go for, putting maximal obstacles in the opponent's way. } ( { In contrast, the passive } 1. Rd3 $6 { is met by } Rh3 2. Ke2 Rg3 $1 ( { not } 2. Re1 Rxd3 3. Kxd3 Kg4 4. Kc4 $1 { and the d-pawn saves a draw; } ) 
 { when White must play } 3. Rd1 ( { since } 3. Rxg3 $2 { loses after } Kxg3 4. Ke3 Kg4 5. Kd3 Kf3 { - Furhoff. } ) 
Rxb3 4. Rg1 $1 { returning to the game. } ) 
Rh3+ 2. Ke2 Rxb3 3. Rg1 $1 ( { The route to d6 matters: after } 3. Rc1 $2 Kg4 $1 4. Rc6 Kf4 5. Rxd6 Kxe4 { Black's active king decides the outcome. } ) 
Rb4 ( { It seems that Black should have played } 3... Kh5 $1 { instead, forcing White's rook to attack the d6-pawn from a worse angle. After } 4. Rg8 Rb4 5. Rd8 Kg5 $1 { the swift activation of the king assures victory, but only through a very precise sequence of moves: } 6. Rxd6 Kf4 $1 7. Rc6 Rxe4 $1 8. Kf2 Rxa4 9. Rxb6 Ra2 $1 10. Ke1 a4 $1 11. d6 Ke3 $1 { etc. Certainly not easy to see all this at the board - one can definitely say that Szabo's approach was the right choice in practice. } ) 
4. Rg6 $1 ( { Sticking to the plan. In the game White instead played } 4. Ke3 $2 { , and after } Rxa4 5. Rg6 Ra3 6. Ke2 ( 6. Kd2 { makes Black's task even easier following } Rb3 7. Rxd6 Kg5 8. Rd8 Kf4 9. Rb8 Kxe4 { when } 10. d6 { runs into } Rd3 { with check } ) 
 { Kotronias could have decided the battle by the prophylactic } b5 $1 { , waiting for } 7. Rxd6 { in order to be able to approach the king by } Kg5 { . At this point, } 8. Re6 Kf4 { is not meaningful for White, while } 8. Rb6 b4 9. d6 Kf6 $1 { lets Black control the d-pawn without losing track of the queenside pawns. In conclusion, an active king is paramount for success with Black in this example. Now back to the main line. } ) 
Rxe4+ 5. Kf3 $1 { Avoiding the check on d4. } Rxa4 6. Rxd6 { White is now two pawns down, but the passed d-pawn is a great asset, and contrary to the previous sublines, Black's king is far away from the action. } Rb4 ( { In the event of } 6... Kg5 7. Rxb6 Kf5 { White should avoid } 8. d6 $2 Ra3 $1 { (a typical intermediate check in rook endgames, here to avoid the white king from reaching e4) } 9. Ke2 Ke6 { when Black would win by pushing both pawns and giving away the e-pawn at the right moment. } ) 
 ( { Preferable is something like } 7... Rb4 7. Ke3 e4 9. d6 Ra3 10. Ke2 $1 { when Philidor's defence is in the air, e.g. } Kf4 11. d7 Rd3 12. Rf6 $1 Ke5 13. Ra6 { and 14.Rxa5. } ) 
7. Re6 { White is just in time to eliminate the e-pawn before Black's king gets back. } Kg5 ( { The more straightforward } 7... a4 8. Rxe5 a3 9. d6 { is even dangerous for Black, e.g. } a2 $4 10. d7 $1 { when White either queens with check or deflects Black's rook after } Rd4 11. Re4 { . } ) 
8. Rxe5+ Kf6 9. Re1 { Other rook moves will also work, but this seems most practical. } a4 10. d6 Rd4 11. Rb1 $1 Rxd6 12. Rb4 ( { After } 12. Ke4 Ke7 13. Rb4 Rh6 $1 { the game gets a bit more tricky. } ) 
Ke5 ( { Similar play arises after } 12... a3 13. Ke2 Ke6 14. Ra4 Kd7 15. Rxa3 Kc6 16. Ra1 $1 { initiating a frontal attack. } ) 
13. Rxa4 Kd5 14. Ke3 Kc5 15. Ra1 $1 { Due to White's cut-off king a frontal attack is the right plan here. } b5 16. Rc1+ { White makes a draw. } 
