 ( { White's pawn minus is well compensated by his advanced protected passed pawn and the active rook. Having just played Rh7-c7, he is now threatening } 1... g4 1. Rxc6 { , giving rise to a pair of connected passed pawns. What should Black do in response? } ) 
 { -KEY- } Re4+ $2 { I made this move almost without any thinking, since it is the only one that keeps the c6-pawn on the board. However, on the sixth rank the rook becomes passive and no longer restricts the movements of the enemy king. } ( { a) Let's see the game continuation: } 1... Re4 2. Kd2 Re6 3. Kc3 Rh6 4. Kb4 Rh4 5. Ka5 Rc4 6. Rxc6 d4 7. Kxa6 d3 8. Rh6 Rxc5 9. b7 Rc6 10. Rxc6 { and Black resigned. Where did I go wrong? Was this perhaps a logical conclusion of a lost endgame? Of course that is not so. In this endgame Black draws, and quite comfortably, as long as he applies an active defence. } ) 
 ( { b) In contrast, the active defence was realized by } 1... Rc4 $1 2. Rxc6 a5 { The white rook is now tied to the c-pawn, so it won't be able to switch angles. After } 3. Rc7 a4 4. c6 a3 { White can't progress anymore, while the a-pawn has become a threat, thus he has to settle for } 5. Rb7 Kc8 ( { of course not } 5... Ka8 $4 6. Ra7 Kb8 7. c7 Kc8 8. Ra8 Kd7 9. Rd8 { etc. } ) 
6. Rc7 Kb8 { with a perpetual. } ) 
 ( { c) } 1... a5 2. Rxc6 Rc4 $1 { is just as good } ) 
2. Kd2 Re6 $2 { A logical follow-up to the previous move. } ( { a) Evidently, the active } 2... Rc4 $1 { was still available; e.g. } 3. Rxc6 a5 { with similar play as in the last note. } ) 
 ( { b) If you prefer a more complex way of making a draw, there is also } 2... a5 3. Rxc6 a4 $5 ( { of course } 3... Rc4 { is much simpler; } ) 
4. Rd6 Rb4 $1 { (preventing c5-c6) } 5. Rxd5 ( { or } 4... Re6 4. Kc3 Rc4 ) 
a3 6. Rd3 a2 7. Ra3 Rc4 ( { or } 7... Rb5 { and Black holds. } ) 
 ) 
3. Kc3 Rh6 $2 { While Black's two previous moves at least carried an idea - defend the c-pawn - this move can only be classified as indifferent. Here I should have asked myself what would happen if the white king reaches a6 - would I be in danger, or would everything still be under control? } ( { Having replied to that question, you quickly come up with the right idea: } 3... Re4 $1 4. Rxc6 Rc4 5. Kb3 a5 { in similar style to the previous two notes. White's improved king doesn't make much difference; e.g. } 6. Rc7 a4 7. Ka3 d4 8. c6 d3 { when a perpetual with } 9. Rb7 Kc8 10. Rc7 Kb8 { is again called for. } ) 
4. Kb4 ( { The king is now close to arriving at a6 , after which White would win by force; e.g. } 4. Kb4 Rg6 5. Ka5 Rh6 6. Kxa6 Rg6 7. Rb7 Ka8 8. Ra7 Kb8 9. b7 { followed by 10.Ra8+. This could be considered as the refutation of the passive defence applied in the game. Curiously, even at this moment Black can still save a draw, by a precise active defence. } ) 
Rh4+ $2 { This check wastes a crucial tempo. } ( { a) The active } 4... Rh1 { preparing a rear attack combined with an advance of the a-pawn, also loses after } 5. Rxc6 Rb1 6. Kc3 a5 7. Kd4 a4 8. Rc7 $1 { and on the next move 9 Ra7 decides, now that White's king supports the passed pawns, and can hide on c6 if necessary. } ) 
 ( { b) But the same plan can be performed with a different move order, first pushing the pawns: } 4... d4 $1 5. Kc4 ( { neither } 4... Rh4+ 4. Rd7 Rh1 6. Rxd4 Rh7 $1 { followed by 7...Kb7 and waiting moves with the rook along the seventh rank; } ) 
 ( { nor } 4... Rh4+ 4. Ka5 Rh1 6. Rxc6 d3 7. Rd6 Ra1 8. Kb4 Rb1 { intending } 9. Kc4 ( { or } 8... Rh4+ 8. Ka3 Rb5 ) 
Rc1 { leads anywhere for White. } ) 
a5 $1 6. Kxd4 a4 7. Kc3 a3 8. Kb3 ( { or } 7... Rh4+ 7. Ra7 Rh3 9. Kb4 Rh4 10. Kxa3 Rc4 $1 11. Ra5 Kb7 { and White can't make progress due to his badly placed rook; e.g. } 12. Kb3 Rc1 13. Kb4 Rb1 14. Kc3 Rc1 15. Kd2 Rc4 16. Ke3 Rc1 17. Kd3 Kb8 $1 18. Kd4 Rd1 19. Ke5 Rd5 20. Ke6 Kb7 { etc. } ) 
Rh3 9. Ka2 { At this point, mutual zugzwangs are around the corner, so Black must be careful: } Rg3 $1 ( { rook moves to f3, e3 or d3 also work, as well as } 9... Rc3 10. Rxc6 Kb7 11. Rc7 Ka8 $1 { only not 11...Kb8? 12 c6 with zugzwang; } ) 
10. Rxc6 Rc3 11. Rc7 Ka8 $1 12. Ra7 { (attempting to liberate the rook) } ( { Now if } 11... Rh4+ 11. c6 { then } Kb8 { and the previous zugzwang is applied upon White, who can't progress; e.g. } 13. Kb1 Rc4 14. Ka1 Rc1 15. Ka2 Rc3 ) 
Kb8 13. Ra5 { Black could reply } Kb7 14. Rb5 Kc8 $1 ( { avoiding } 14... Kb8 $2 15. b7 { with zugzwang; } ) 
15. b7 Kb8 { with yet another zugzwang; e.g. } 16. Kb1 Rc4 17. Ka1 Rc1 18. Ka2 Rc3 { and nothing has changed. Evidently, such variations are much easier to work out after than during the game. From a practical perspective, Black's main mistakes were committed earlier. } ) 
5. Ka5 Rc4 { Ironically, Black applies the correct idea when it is already late. } ( { In any case he was short of a proper defence; e.g. } 5... Rh1 6. Rxc6 Ra1 7. Kb4 Rb1 ( { or } 7... a5 8. Kb5 { heading for a6; } ) 
8. Kc3 a5 9. Kd4 a4 10. Rc7 $1 { heading for a7, a position that we are familiar with from the note to Black's fourth move. } ( { Incidentally, } 9... Rc4 9. Rg6 a3 11. Kxd5 a2 12. Kc6 { also works for White. } ) 
 ) 
6. Rxc6 d4 7. Kxa6 d3 8. Rh6 $1 { A decisive switch of angle. } Rxc5 9. b7 Rc6+ { A joke in a lost position. } 10. Rxc6 
